00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.290
- Today what I'm gonna talk
about is a research paper,

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:05.510
a review article,

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:10.510
talking about the origin
of the new coronavirus

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:12.510
that's wreaking havoc everywhere,

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:16.023
and essentially asking the
question where did it come from?

00:00:20.205 --> 00:00:24.270
And as previously mentioned,
this is a review article,

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.140
so this is summarizing

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:31.140
around 30 different research
articles from different fields

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:34.250
and different types of research,

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:37.210
and I'm gonna talk about many of those,

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:40.180
but first I just wanted to
give you an introduction to me

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:42.460
and kind of my scientific past.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:47.370
So I'm originally from
Virginia, and where I grew up,

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:52.370
but after college I moved to New York

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:54.770
and stayed with my grandmother

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:59.300
here in this nice cozy
New York City apartment,

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.630
and during that time I also

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:05.823
luckily found a career
in scientific research,

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:10.080
and so over the past 10 years or so

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.290
I've been studying and working in a lab,

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:17.153
and so this is, well let
me switch to the pointer,

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:22.540
and so this is me in a lab,

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:27.540
and I typically study
things related to DNA,

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.513
and right now I'm studying how
DNA is organized in a cell.

00:01:33.596 --> 00:01:36.840
So as you all might know
DNA, it's really long,

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:39.150
so if you took a single cell

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:41.400
and stretched out all the DNA,

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.480
it would actually be much taller than me,

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:47.040
about the size of Michael
Jordan it would be,

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.490
and all that DNA has to
fit in a very tiny cell,

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:54.120
and it organizes itself in different ways.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.180
And that's what I'm currently studying

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:57.913
at Rockefeller University,

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:03.870
but what I'm curious about also is

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:09.930
how DNA and other genetic
information give rise

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:13.020
to evolution and different traits,

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:14.360
and I'm gonna be talking about that

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:19.333
in light of the viral
evolution of the coronavirus.

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:24.890
Okay so this is me
currently with my partner

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:27.930
in our cozy New York City apartment.

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:32.930
I'm trying to maintain our
mental health by doing some yoga.

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:38.070
I've also been doing some
sewing projects as well,

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:40.550
and I don't know if you all can see this

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:44.270
but there's a cookie inside of this mug.

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:45.960
So we've been doing a lot of baking

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.440
and a couple of cookies a day is

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:52.373
also helping keep our spirits
up during these trying times.

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:56.410
Okay so now to the research.

00:02:56.410 --> 00:03:01.280
So as the, as Jeanne mentioned,

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.860
the name of this research is
called the proximal origin

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:08.070
of SARS-CoV-2, or as I'll refer to it

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:10.470
as the new coronavirus,

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:13.180
and this question that
they're asking is essentially

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:17.690
when did the new Coronavirus,
as pictured in a cartoon here,

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:19.523
become deadly to humans?

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.630
And again this is a
correspondence or review article,

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:27.340
but there are still many different authors

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:29.180
from different parts of the world

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:33.260
that have collaborated together to write

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:36.900
and critically think about this topic,

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.210
but you also notice something

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:42.410
quiet outstanding here is that

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:47.410
this article has been viewed
4.2, almost 4.3 million times

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:51.210
by different people throughout the world

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:53.370
in a very short time,

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:55.160
and one of the reasons for this is

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:58.040
because of the implications

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:03.040
that this article talks
about is whether or not

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:08.440
this new coronavirus was
a result of bioterrorism,

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:11.330
which has been debunked but,

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:15.050
and their rationale I'll
present to you today,

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:17.480
but also I wanna present
to you the rationale

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:20.180
of where they think it
might have come from

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:22.570
and became deadly to humans.

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:25.520
I also want to kind of
tell you the techniques

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:29.340
that allow us to track genetic changes.

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:32.703
So that's also part of
the goal of this talk,

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:36.490
and I just wanna reemphasize that

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:39.730
even though the authors
didn't do the research,

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:42.500
they did do a lot of,

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:43.990
there are many authors

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:46.450
and they are coming from
different parts of the world,

00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:49.050
a couple within the United States

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:51.530
but there is also some in Australia

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:54.290
and in Edinburgh University of UK,

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:57.020
but a lot of the research
has been done in China

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:58.980
but also different parts of the world.

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:02.610
So this is really a collaborative event

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:05.030
and just highlighting that

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:10.030
in science it's such a important
thing to do, cooperate.

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:15.840
So before we get into talking
about the actual research

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.240
and the discussion underlying it,

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.340
I wanna go over a few key terms.

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:24.090
So I know the central dogma was reviewed

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:26.080
in this series last week,

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:30.200
but I also want to review it just briefly,

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:31.970
and then also I'm gonna be talking

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:36.667
about an important region
of the coronavirus protein

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:40.460
called the receptor-binding domain.

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:41.590
I'll also be talking about

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:44.040
this technique called sequence alignment

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:48.990
and how that is used
to watch the evolution,

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:51.723
how we track the evolution
of the virus through that,

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:55.853
and using this idea of a molecular clock.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:00.510
Okay so we would have our virus here,

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:04.120
and inside the virus there
is this gene material,

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:07.080
or genome as I might
refer to it sometimes,

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:10.730
and this in the virus is an RNA molecule.

00:06:10.730 --> 00:06:14.180
In humans this is a DNA molecule,

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:16.110
but the virus has RNA,

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:21.110
and this material is the founding
material that gets started

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:26.030
and tells it's the information
how to build the cell.

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:30.440
This material then gets
transcribed, essentially copied,

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:33.020
like you would just transcribe a note,

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:35.280
into what we call messenger RNA,

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:38.230
which is just an intermediate stage

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:42.450
that allows the cell to
take this information

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:44.520
and then convert it into protein,

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:46.610
which is a really important part,

00:06:46.610 --> 00:06:48.400
and this is called translation,

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:51.280
and the reason this is
called translation is because

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:53.970
it's in some sense a different language,

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:56.910
even though it's directly based off of

00:06:56.910 --> 00:07:00.420
this RNA language that it's coming from,

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:03.700
and this language has what we refer to

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:05.520
as different kind of letters

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:07.940
or we refer to it as amino acids.

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:11.680
So these are just abbreviations
for those amino acids.

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:16.460
So you can see that Y-H-T-V-S,
they're just shorthand,

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:20.490
and they're organized in
this kind of linear string,

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:25.490
and these encode for the very
functional parts of the cell,

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:31.410
the proteins that execute the tasks.

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:33.980
So for example you all might have heard

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:37.900
of a spike domain or a spike protein,

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:39.560
and so this is important

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:42.640
for the infectiousness of the virus,

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:46.140
and I'm gonna talk a little
bit more about this protein

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:48.033
in its different domains,

00:07:49.450 --> 00:07:52.870
but before that I just
also want to remind you all

00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:57.870
that this genetic element
here, the RNA of the virus,

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:00.900
it needs to be able to replicate itself,

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:02.950
and during this replication

00:08:02.950 --> 00:08:05.560
this can sometimes cause mutations,

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:09.320
and these mutations
generally happen randomly,

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:11.860
but essentially like making it an error,

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:14.890
and these errors can be propagated

00:08:14.890 --> 00:08:19.890
and continued on into the
next virus, passed along.

00:08:20.030 --> 00:08:24.760
Okay, so we have our protein words

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:28.360
made out of our amino
acid so-called letters,

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:32.780
but these can be expanded on
just like we can create words

00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:36.930
into sentences and give
it more information,

00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:39.963
and so for proteins how
this works is that they,

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:45.193
this linear organization
of the amino acids

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:51.030
then can fold into different
structures and shapes

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:54.993
that allow it to give it
different tasks within the cell,

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:59.360
and this can be even more complicated

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:03.930
and give more context
to how this domain works

00:09:04.830 --> 00:09:07.810
with different parts or
different domains of a protein

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:11.330
or with different even more proteins.

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:13.950
And so what I'm showing
here this green one

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:17.430
this is actually the
receptor-binding domain

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:21.970
that's found in the coronavirus,
the new coronavirus,

00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:26.330
and this domain is really
important for its ability

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:31.330
to interact with cells that
express this ACE-2 receptor.

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:36.290
So these cells include the human lungs

00:09:36.290 --> 00:09:37.540
and other different tissues,

00:09:37.540 --> 00:09:39.910
but the lungs are one of
the most prevalent one

00:09:39.910 --> 00:09:42.310
that express this receptor,

00:09:42.310 --> 00:09:46.580
and this receptor-binding
domain, as the name suggests,

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:49.260
helps it bind to the cell

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:52.480
and then allows it eventually to interact

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:55.173
or to enter the cell
and become infectious.

00:09:56.010 --> 00:10:00.800
So the way we think about these
different shapes are kind of

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:04.930
like this very classic toy
in which you have shapes

00:10:04.930 --> 00:10:09.320
that corresponds to different receptors.

00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:13.840
So for example this one
here would fit nicely

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:15.360
in this small hole,

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:19.110
whereas it might fit in these other ones

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:23.420
but it might not be as
snug or as compatible,

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:25.890
so it might fall out more easily.

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:27.190
So that's kind of how we think

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:29.690
about proteins and their shapes

00:10:29.690 --> 00:10:34.690
and how that informs different
interactions in the cell.

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:39.120
Okay, and so now the technique

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:41.660
that is most used and talked about

00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:43.940
in this research review is

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:47.340
this technique called sequence alignment,

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:52.170
and this can be done with
the RNA sequence, the genes,

00:10:52.170 --> 00:10:53.590
but what we're gonna talk

00:10:53.590 --> 00:10:57.000
about is just the protein sequence,

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:00.310
and so the goal of this is just,

00:11:00.310 --> 00:11:05.200
it's similar to a search
function or a find.

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:07.770
If you wanna find all the
things that are similar,

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:11.320
they don't necessarily have
to be the exact sequence,

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:15.680
but you can see that you
wanna line the sequences up

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:18.530
so that they are most similar.

00:11:18.530 --> 00:11:21.143
Even though there are
some differences here.

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:26.300
Yeah, so essentially just finding things

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:29.100
that are similar or related to each other,

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:32.810
and one of the things that
this does is allows us

00:11:32.810 --> 00:11:37.810
to identify those amino acids,
those that stay the same,

00:11:39.010 --> 00:11:42.360
and those might give us some
insight into the structure

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:45.400
and what kind of structures are important

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:49.700
for the protein to
create different shapes,

00:11:49.700 --> 00:11:50.940
but also what things

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:54.950
that the virus might
need to always maintain.

00:11:54.950 --> 00:11:59.950
Some other ones that are more
mutated and changed are often

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:03.680
those that can evolve and
take different shapes,

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:05.620
and those also might be important

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:09.877
for understanding the
evolution of the protein

00:12:11.147 --> 00:12:12.533
and the virus in general,

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:18.930
and so what we can do is
use this alignment technique

00:12:19.390 --> 00:12:24.380
to essentially map the
what we call as a lineage,

00:12:24.380 --> 00:12:29.380
but is essentially trying to
understand who came first,

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:33.860
and then who gave rise to the
next one and the next one,

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:36.760
just like we think about
our great grandparents,

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:39.730
our grandparents, and then
all the way down to the child.

00:12:39.730 --> 00:12:43.160
So the way this works is essentially

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:46.590
those sequences that are most similar

00:12:46.590 --> 00:12:51.590
and those that we think are
the most distant ancestors

00:12:51.870 --> 00:12:56.870
or relatives, and then we
look at the difference.

00:12:57.950 --> 00:13:00.840
The next one we'll map is the one

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:03.250
that has only a slight difference.

00:13:03.250 --> 00:13:06.350
So in this case this S changed to a T,

00:13:06.350 --> 00:13:09.350
but otherwise it's the same sequence,

00:13:09.350 --> 00:13:13.730
and so this is likely to be the next step

00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:15.580
of a mutation that occurred,

00:13:15.580 --> 00:13:20.580
but this T is maintained
throughout the rest of these,

00:13:23.740 --> 00:13:28.740
is passed on, and then other
mutations can build off

00:13:29.810 --> 00:13:33.200
of that and create more changes,

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:37.673
and so this is the way we
track time essentially,

00:13:39.070 --> 00:13:42.530
in a genetic, or in a genetic lineage

00:13:42.530 --> 00:13:46.713
because proteins are ultimately
coming from the genes.

00:13:48.030 --> 00:13:51.980
Okay, so now we reviewed these key terms.

00:13:51.980 --> 00:13:54.500
We have the central dogma,

00:13:54.500 --> 00:13:58.610
which is essentially going
from genes to proteins,

00:13:58.610 --> 00:14:01.950
and these proteins are
functioning in the cell

00:14:01.950 --> 00:14:04.630
and have like tasks,

00:14:04.630 --> 00:14:08.060
and the one protein domain
that we talked about

00:14:08.060 --> 00:14:11.090
of the coronavirus is this
receptor-binding domain,

00:14:11.090 --> 00:14:13.280
which is really important for its ability

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:18.150
to interact with some
of the cells in humans,

00:14:18.150 --> 00:14:20.590
in the ACE2 receptor,

00:14:20.590 --> 00:14:24.470
and then we talked about
the sequence alignment,

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:27.850
which is essentially just
identifying who's related,

00:14:27.850 --> 00:14:30.670
who's in the family, who's
coming to the cookout.

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:35.670
The next is a molecular clock
using this sequence alignment

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:39.500
to essentially trace lineage
and see who came first,

00:14:39.500 --> 00:14:43.623
who's the intermediate, and
who's the most recent one.

00:14:44.650 --> 00:14:47.640
Okay, and because this is a review article

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:51.270
that's encompassing many
different types of research,

00:14:51.270 --> 00:14:52.103
I'm just gonna go

00:14:52.103 --> 00:14:54.950
over the different styles
of scientific reasoning

00:14:54.950 --> 00:14:57.830
because there are many types of ways

00:14:57.830 --> 00:14:59.630
to create scientific knowledge.

00:14:59.630 --> 00:15:03.390
So one of the first ways
is this categorization

00:15:03.390 --> 00:15:05.180
and classification,

00:15:05.180 --> 00:15:07.030
and in this paper essentially

00:15:07.030 --> 00:15:10.420
what that means is collecting the genes,

00:15:10.420 --> 00:15:12.800
the genomes of different viruses, okay.

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:17.800
So sequencing the genes and
compiling them, and then

00:15:18.160 --> 00:15:21.010
there's the historical-based
evolutionary reasoning.

00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:23.660
So these are different ways of thinking

00:15:23.660 --> 00:15:27.030
that have been established
in the evolutionary field

00:15:27.030 --> 00:15:31.330
that give us ways to interpret the data

00:15:31.330 --> 00:15:32.810
that I'll talk about,

00:15:32.810 --> 00:15:36.710
and then lastly there's
this hypothetical modeling,

00:15:36.710 --> 00:15:41.710
which is essentially taking
computer math equations

00:15:42.730 --> 00:15:47.730
that build off each other
and use different scenarios

00:15:49.570 --> 00:15:54.500
to calculate different possible things.

00:15:54.500 --> 00:15:59.050
So you've probably heard this
in the context of modeling

00:15:59.050 --> 00:16:01.700
for the amount of cases of coronavirus

00:16:01.700 --> 00:16:06.700
that are gonna happen in
different populations,

00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:10.590
and those can be changing
depending on human behavior.

00:16:10.590 --> 00:16:13.190
What I wanted to talk about
is hypothetical modeling

00:16:14.860 --> 00:16:17.003
of proteins and the structure of proteins,

00:16:18.780 --> 00:16:22.340
but first I just wanna
review that the overall goal

00:16:22.340 --> 00:16:25.050
of this study was to determine

00:16:25.050 --> 00:16:27.670
how the new coronavirus emerged

00:16:27.670 --> 00:16:30.083
and when it became deadly in humans.

00:16:31.310 --> 00:16:35.540
Okay so the authors discussed
three possibilities.

00:16:35.540 --> 00:16:40.540
So one is that the coronavirus
originated in animals,

00:16:41.300 --> 00:16:45.430
and that the current deadly form

00:16:45.430 --> 00:16:49.870
to humans originated in
animals and jumped to humans,

00:16:49.870 --> 00:16:53.220
and then began to spread
