00:28:46.480 --> 00:28:50.100
So from the sequence alignment,

00:28:50.100 --> 00:28:53.980
just the uncanny similarity
between those found in nature

00:28:55.280 --> 00:28:57.700
really suggests that it is a product

00:28:57.700 --> 00:29:01.210
of evolution in humans or in animals,

00:29:01.210 --> 00:29:03.573
or starting at least in animals,

00:29:04.870 --> 00:29:09.260
but there's also this really
important reason is that

00:29:09.260 --> 00:29:13.300
because viruses, they don't, we can't,

00:29:13.300 --> 00:29:16.210
it's not very easy to isolate a virus

00:29:16.210 --> 00:29:19.210
because there are also
many different viruses

00:29:19.210 --> 00:29:21.960
within one cell or one sample,

00:29:21.960 --> 00:29:26.710
and so when we isolate
the viruses in the lab,

00:29:28.290 --> 00:29:33.290
we often add these DNA signatures to them,

00:29:33.420 --> 00:29:38.420
and these DNAs or in this
case it would be RNA, sorry,

00:29:38.520 --> 00:29:43.520
but we add these genes to them
that allow us to track them

00:29:43.840 --> 00:29:46.670
and manipulate them and
make them very easy,

00:29:46.670 --> 00:29:51.670
and otherwise it's really very
difficult to work with those

00:29:51.670 --> 00:29:54.690
and edit them or manipulate them in ways

00:29:54.690 --> 00:29:58.730
that would be necessary
for a bioterrorism agent.

00:29:58.730 --> 00:30:01.980
So all the sequences that have been found

00:30:01.980 --> 00:30:05.700
thus far lack any kind of signature

00:30:05.700 --> 00:30:07.023
that is found in the lab,

00:30:08.060 --> 00:30:10.760
both in humans or in animals.

00:30:10.760 --> 00:30:13.300
So none of those exist.

00:30:13.300 --> 00:30:17.790
Another reason is using modeling data

00:30:17.790 --> 00:30:20.820
for the protein structure
and the interaction

00:30:20.820 --> 00:30:25.820
between the receptor-binding
domain and the ACE2 receptor.

00:30:26.690 --> 00:30:31.380
So what researchers found
out is that the structure

00:30:31.380 --> 00:30:34.670
and the shapes of the
receptor-binding domain

00:30:34.670 --> 00:30:37.250
that are found in the new coronavirus,

00:30:37.250 --> 00:30:41.830
even though they are very
infectious and make everyone sick,

00:30:41.830 --> 00:30:45.680
they're more like taking
one of these little circles

00:30:45.680 --> 00:30:50.680
and putting it in this big hole
where it doesn't really fit.

00:30:50.788 --> 00:30:53.570
It can fit there but it's not really snug,

00:30:53.570 --> 00:30:55.740
and so they suggest if that

00:30:55.740 --> 00:30:59.300
somebody were to create
a bioterrorism agent

00:30:59.300 --> 00:31:03.650
they would create something
that is more snuggly fit,

00:31:03.650 --> 00:31:06.330
more efficient at doing its job

00:31:06.330 --> 00:31:11.220
and becoming more infectious.

00:31:11.220 --> 00:31:14.270
Okay, and so the general conclusions are

00:31:14.270 --> 00:31:17.070
that the new coronavirus has a signature

00:31:17.070 --> 00:31:19.610
that's occurring in nature.

00:31:19.610 --> 00:31:20.840
It has the highest sequence

00:31:20.840 --> 00:31:24.550
similarity to a virus found in bats.

00:31:24.550 --> 00:31:29.550
It has a receptor-binding
domain signature of a virus

00:31:29.890 --> 00:31:33.620
that's found in pangolins,
and this is quite recent,

00:31:33.620 --> 00:31:37.570
this virus, infecting in the pangolin.

00:31:37.570 --> 00:31:40.310
It has a cleavage site

00:31:40.310 --> 00:31:42.270
that's also important for its function,

00:31:42.270 --> 00:31:47.270
but this is more recent and
